    "markdown": "---\ntitle: Multi-response models\nguide-section: \"Advanced features\"\nbread-crumbs: false\n---\n\n\n\nWhen multiple outcomes are measured on the same observations — for example, blood pressure and\ncholesterol, or multiple pollutant concentrations at the same monitoring stations — fitting them\njointly can capture shared structure and estimate residual correlations. Whittaker's\n`MultiResponseGAM` fits a separate GAM per response but provides a unified interface for prediction,\ndiagnostics, and correlation estimation.\n\n## Basic multi-response model\n\n::: {#5b5ab91c .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport numpy as np\nimport whittaker as wk\n\n# Generate two correlated responses driven by the same smooth\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nn = 300\nx = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, n)\n\ne1 = rng.normal(0, 0.3, n)\ne2 = 0.7 * e1 + rng.normal(0, 0.2, n)  # correlated errors\n\ny1 = np.sin(x) + e1\ny2 = 0.5 * np.sin(x) + 1.5 + e2\n\ndata = {\"x\": x, \"y1\": y1, \"y2\": y2}\n```\n:::\n\n\nThe two responses share the same smooth predictor `x` but have different signal strengths and\ncorrelated residuals. We fit them jointly:\n\n::: {#c6e6f76e .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n# Fit a multi-response GAM with a shared smooth formula\nmodel = wk.MultiResponseGAM([\"y1\", \"y2\"], \"s(x)\")\nmodel.fit(data, method=\"REML\")\n\nprint(f\"Responses: {model.responses}\")\nprint(f\"N responses: {model.n_responses}\")\nprint(f\"Is fitted: {model.is_fitted}\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nResponses: ['y1', 'y2']\nN responses: 2\nIs fitted: True\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe first argument lists the response column names. The second is the shared formula (covariates\nonly, no response side). Each response gets its own GAM with the same smooth structure.\n\n## Predictions\n\n::: {#2b439e55 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n# Predict both responses on new data\nx_new = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100)\nresult = model.predict({\"x\": x_new})\n\n# Access predictions by response name\nprint(f\"y1 predictions shape: {result['y1'].values.shape}\")\nprint(f\"y2 predictions shape: {result['y2'].values.shape}\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\ny1 predictions shape: (100,)\ny2 predictions shape: (100,)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#7ba38796 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport altair as alt\n\n# Plot both response fits\nplot_data = []\nfor i in range(len(x_new)):\n    plot_data.append({\"x\": float(x_new[i]), \"y\": float(result[\"y1\"].values[i]), \"response\": \"y1\"})\n    plot_data.append({\"x\": float(x_new[i]), \"y\": float(result[\"y2\"].values[i]), \"response\": \"y2\"})\n\nalt.Chart({\"values\": plot_data}).mark_line(strokeWidth=2).encode(\n    x=alt.X(\"x:Q\"),\n    y=alt.Y(\"y:Q\"),\n    color=alt.Color(\"response:N\"),\n).properties(width=500, height=300, title=\"Multi-response GAM predictions\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n\n<style>\n  #altair-viz-cc611736498a43cbb08904de6e996905.vega-embed {\n    width: 100%;\n    display: flex;\n  }\n\n  #altair-viz-cc611736498a43cbb08904de6e996905.vega-embed details,\n  #altair-viz-cc611736498a43cbb08904de6e996905.vega-embed details summary {\n    position: relative;\n  }\n</style>\n<div id=\"altair-viz-cc611736498a43cbb08904de6e996905\"></div>\n<script type=\"text/javascript\">\n  var VEGA_DEBUG = (typeof VEGA_DEBUG == \"undefined\") ? {} : VEGA_DEBUG;\n  (function(spec, embedOpt){\n    let outputDiv = document.currentScript.previousElementSibling;\n    if (outputDiv.id !== \"altair-viz-cc611736498a43cbb08904de6e996905\") {\n      outputDiv = document.getElementById(\"altair-viz-cc611736498a43cbb08904de6e996905\");\n    }\n\n    const paths = {\n      \"vega\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/vega@6?noext\",\n      \"vega-lib\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/vega-lib?noext\",\n      \"vega-lite\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm